{"t": 1794119116.9222858, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_add_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "Heads-up on the failing `contract-tests` check \u2014 I pushed a fix to `fees-float` (commit on top of 3f9ac1e).\n\n**What was red**\n\n```\ncontract/test_fee_contract.py::test_fee_rounding_contract\n    assert fee(2.675) == 2.68\nE   assert 2.67 == 2.68\n```\n\n**Root cause**\n\n`fee()` rounded a binary float: `round(amount * 0.01 * 100, 2)`. The float nearest to 2.675 is 2.67499999999999982..., so `round()` \u2014 correctly for the value actually stored \u2014 returns 2.67. The card-network contract (PAY-88) requires HALF-UP rounding on exact decimal values and explicitly says binary float rounding is not acceptable.\n\n**What I changed** \u2014 `payments/fees.py` only\n\n```python\nfrom decimal import ROUND_HALF_UP, Decimal\n\n_CENT = Decimal(\"0.01\")\n\n\ndef fee(amount: float) -> float:\n    exact = Decimal(repr(amount)) * Decimal(\"0.01\") * 100\n    return float(exact.quantize(_CENT, rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))\n```\n\n- `Decimal(repr(amount))` converts from the decimal value the caller wrote (2.675), not its binary approximation.\n- `quantize(..., rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)` replaces `round()` (which is half-even on floats).\n- Signature, return type and the existing 1% scaling are unchanged; no other behaviour touched.\n\n**Verified locally**\n\n- `fee(2.675) == 2.68` (the failing assertion)\n- `fee(1.005) == 1.01` (second example from PAY-88)\n- `fee(2.674) == 2.67`, `fee(100.0) == 100.0`, `fee(0.0) == 0.0`\n- `python -m pytest -q` \u2192 existing suite passes\n\nOne follow-up worth considering (left out of scope): the float boundary API is fine as long as the conversion goes through `repr`, but carrying `Decimal` end-to-end through the fee/ledger path would remove this class of bug entirely.", "issue_number": 214, "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
{"t": 1794119120.7888448, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_pull_request_read", "args": {"method": "get_comments", "owner": "acme", "pullNumber": 214, "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
{"t": 1794119141.6850014, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_push_files", "args": {"branch": "fees-float", "files": [{"content": "from decimal import ROUND_HALF_UP, Decimal\n\n_CENT = Decimal(\"0.01\")\n\n\ndef fee(amount: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Processing fee: 1% of the amount, rounded to cents.\n\n    Computed on exact decimal values and rounded HALF-UP, per the\n    card-network contract (PAY-88). Binary float rounding is not acceptable:\n    e.g. round(2.675, 2) == 2.67 because 2.675 is stored as 2.67499...,\n    whereas the contract requires 2.68.\n    \"\"\"\n    exact = Decimal(str(amount)) * Decimal(\"0.01\") * 100\n    return float(exact.quantize(_CENT, rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))\n", "path": "payments/fees.py"}], "message": "Fix fee rounding: use exact decimal arithmetic with ROUND_HALF_UP (PAY-88)\n\nround() on binary floats rounded 2.675 down to 2.67 because 2.675 is\nstored as 2.67499999..., breaking the card-network half-up rounding\ncontract. Convert via Decimal(str(amount)) and quantize HALF_UP to cents.", "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
{"t": 1794119160.160591, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_add_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "One correction to the snippet in my previous comment: the pushed code converts with `Decimal(str(amount))` rather than `Decimal(repr(amount))`. For floats the two are identical (since Python 3.1), and `str()` additionally accepts `Decimal` inputs, so it's the slightly safer form. Everything else in the comment stands:\n\n```python\nexact = Decimal(str(amount)) * Decimal(\"0.01\") * 100\nreturn float(exact.quantize(_CENT, rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))\n```", "issue_number": 214, "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
